WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.712
Modern detection engineering treats detections as living assets.

2
00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:14.468
A detection is proposed, designed, tested, deployed, documented, measured, tuned, reviewed, and eventually retired when it no longer provides enough value.

3
00:00:14.468 --> 00:00:17.391
The lifecycle begins with a reason for the detection.

4
00:00:17.391 --> 00:00:28.566
That reason may come from threat intelligence, a prior incident, a hunting hypothesis, a control requirement, a purple team exercise, or a known visibility gap.

5
00:00:28.566 --> 00:00:33.645
Design work connects the reason to observable behavior and available telemetry.

6
00:00:33.645 --> 00:00:41.797
Engineers decide what fields are needed, how the logic should work, what context should be included, and how analysts should interpret the result.

7
00:00:41.797 --> 00:00:44.890
Testing and deployment help prevent avoidable mistakes.

8
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:53.580
Teams validate that a detection fires when expected, avoids obvious noise where possible, and presents useful information to the people who will use it.

9
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.779
Maintenance is the part that separates a durable program from a collection of old alerts.

10
00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:04.965
Environments change, log schemas change, business processes change, and threat patterns change.

11
00:01:04.965 --> 00:01:08.487
Mature teams review detections as part of normal operations.

12
00:01:08.487 --> 00:01:11.361
Lifecycle management also clarifies ownership.

13
00:01:11.361 --> 00:01:20.241
Someone should know why a detection exists, which data it depends on, how it was validated, what response guidance applies, and when it should be reviewed again.

14
00:01:20.241 --> 00:01:22.636
Retirement is part of the lifecycle too.

15
00:01:22.636 --> 00:01:34.410
If a detection no longer maps to relevant systems, creates recurring noise, or depends on unavailable data, retiring or redesigning it may be healthier than keeping it for appearance.

16
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.732
A healthy lifecycle creates evidence as well as alerts.

17
00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:46.802
Design notes, test results, review decisions, and tuning history help teams understand how the detection changed and why those changes were made.
